# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: vl-exit-with-error-leaks-host-condition -- vl-exit-with-error outside a VLX crashes with an SBCL backtrace
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-21

* Symptom

Found while writing the functional-abstraction section of
=clautolisp/documentation/tutorial.org=, on clautolisp 2.3.24:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp --no-init -x '(vl-exit-with-error "boom")'
Unhandled CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-RUNTIME:AUTOLISP-NAMESPACE-EXIT in thread #<SB-THREAD:THREAD tid=259 "main thread" RUNNING ...>:
  AutoLISP namespace exit ERROR: "boom"
Backtrace for: #<SB-THREAD:THREAD ...>
0: (SB-DEBUG::DEBUGGER-DISABLED-HOOK ...)
...
unhandled condition in --disable-debugger mode, quitting
$ echo $?
1
#+end_example

Same with a global =*error*= defined: it is not called. Inside
=vl-catch-all-apply= it is caught correctly (=#<catch-all-error "boom">=).

* Expected

AGENTS.md: "Common Lisp conditions and errors must not leak directly into the
AutoLISP environment." =AUTOLISP-NAMESPACE-EXIT= signalled from the document
namespace (no separate VLX namespace to exit) escapes every handler of the
toplevel driver and reaches SBCL's debugger-disabled hook. It must instead be
handled like an AutoLISP error at the toplevel (message "boom", =*error*= /
=--on-error= policy applied), or like whatever the vendors do when
=vl-exit-with-error= is called outside a separate-namespace VLX -- to be
probed (AutoCAD documents it only for separate-namespace VLX applications).

* To do

1. Probe AutoCAD / BricsCAD: =(vl-exit-with-error "boom")= at the command line
   and from a plain =.lsp=; record the message and whether =*error*= runs.
2. Failing test, then handle =AUTOLISP-NAMESPACE-EXIT= at the toplevel (REPL,
   =-x=, =-l=) and in the debugger session.
3. Same check for =vl-exit-with-value=.
